data.content:
## .NET 10 OpenAPI Breaking Changes

Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0 upgrades from **Microsoft.OpenApi v1 → v2**, which targets **OpenAPI v3.1** (based on JSON Schema). Here's what broke and how to fix each piece:

### 1. `OpenApiString` / `OpenApiAny` types removed
OpenAPI v2 uses `System.Text.Json.Nodes.JsonNode` instead of the `IOpenApiAny` hierarchy.

```csharp
// ❌ Old
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Any;
param.Schema.Example = new OpenApiString("1.0");

// ✅ New
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
```

### 2. `OpenApiResponse` in Responses dictionary
`operation.Responses` is now a `Dictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>` — `TryAdd` still works, but make sure you're not using the old `OpenApiResponses` wrapper type anywhere.

```csharp
// ✅ Still works
operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });
```

### 3. `OpenApiReference` + `ReferenceType` changed
`ReferenceType` was replaced with `ReferenceType` string constants, and references are now set differently.

```csharp
// ❌ Old
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "oauth2" }
};

// ✅ New
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "oauth2" }
};
// ReferenceType.SecurityScheme still exists but is now a string-based enum.
// If you get errors, check that your `using` includes Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References
// and consider using the new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference:
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2");
```

### 4. `OpenApiSecurityRequirement` is no longer a `Dictionary`
It no longer inherits from `Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>`.

```csharp
// ❌ Old
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new() { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
};

// ✅ New
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new() { Schemes = new Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>
        { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
    }
};
```

### 5. `Nullable` property removed
OpenAPI 3.1 uses JSON Schema's type array instead of a separate `Nullable` flag. A nullable string is `Type = JsonSchemaType.String | JsonSchemaType.Null`.

```csharp
// ❌ Old
property.Value.Nullable = false;

// ✅ New — remove the Null flag from the type
property.Value.Type = property.Value.Type & ~JsonSchemaType.Null;
```

### Full corrected code

```csharp
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References;

options.AddOperationTransformer((operation, context, ct) =>
{
    var param = operation.Parameters.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "api-version");
    if (param is not null)
    {
        param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
    }

    operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });

    var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2");
    operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
    {
        new() { Schemes = new Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>
            { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
        }
    };

    return Task.CompletedTask;
});

options.AddSchemaTransformer((schema, context, ct) =>
{
    if (schema.Properties is not null)
    {
        foreach (var property in schema.Properties)
        {
            if (schema.Required?.Contains(property.Key) != true)
                property.Value.Type = property.Value.Type & ~JsonSchemaType.Null;
        }
    }
    return Task.CompletedTask;
});
```

The root cause of all these changes is the move to **OpenAPI v3.1 / JSON Schema**, which Microsoft.OpenApi v2 implements. If you hit other issues, check the [Microsoft.OpenApi v2 migration guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/openapi/migration-v2).
